      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T13:12:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ahh ok got it. But now when I think about it, I feel this reference count mechanism is not serving its purpose fully. If a detach is called while some one has taken a refcount, detach returns NULL and the vcpu will not be freed I think. Would that be a problem? If we are sure that detach will be the last operation and no reference count would exist at that time, then its okay. But then, do we really need a refcount in that case?\n\nIMHO It should be the put_vcpu function that should free the resource when refcount is zero. detach could do the detach operation and decrement the refcount. Since detach would reset the vcpus[handle] to NULL, no further get_pkvm_vcpu will succeed and last put operation can free the vcpu gracefully.\n\nI do not have a full background of the issue and might be missing something. Just thinking out loud :-)",
      "parentUuid": "ec89131c_32892f62",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 88,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 88,
        "endChar": 35
      },
      "revId": "0614d355879a522356094e367a4e49d946c47bfa",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2d6292a0_d700fdea",
        "filename": "arch/x86/kvm/pkvm/pkvm.h",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 88,
      "author": {
        "id": 4085392
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-10-14T13:40:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e If a detach is called while some one has taken a refcount, detach returns NULL and the vcpu will not be freed I think. Would that be a problem?\n\nIt should not be a problem as this is what we want to achieve, to prevent the host to take the vcpu memory pages back while the pKVM hypervisor is still using this vcpu (which is indicated by holding its reference count).\n\n\u003e If we are sure that detach will be the last operation and no reference count would exist at that time, then its okay. But then, do we really need a refcount in that case?\n\nWe are not sure when the host will use the vcpu_free PV interface. A normal host may only use the vcpu_free PV interface when no reference count being hold, but a malicious host can use the vcpu_free PV interface at any time.\n\n\u003e IMHO It should be the put_vcpu function that should free the resource when refcount is zero. detach could do the detach operation and decrement the refcount. Since detach would reset the vcpus[handle] to NULL, no further get_pkvm_vcpu will succeed and last put operation can free the vcpu gracefully.\n\nThe reference count by default is 1, means the resource is free. And some one can get this resource and increment the reference count. Change the reference count to zero means the resource is not valid. So getting this resource will be failed in this case, thus no one can use this resource any more. Thus we can free the resource at the moment. After we put the pkvm_vcpu, its reference can decrement to 1 but not zero. So we cannot free the pkvm_vcpu at put. Also, the pkvm_vcpu should be freed to the host, thus we free it when the host wants the pkvm_vcpu memory pages back via the vcpu_free PV interface.",
      "parentUuid": "d226b4b2_65a1e0e5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 88,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 88,
